{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:inflation:audit","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:4baf16ad6c1d0028c7fd6f1a3187fc37cae18d1282246eb0d226eace1f3242b0","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"quantiles","quantiles":[{"probability":0.1,"value":1},{"probability":0.5,"value":2},{"probability":0.9,"value":3.1}]},"questionHash":"sha256:20d8e05d3bea415eb4452ff8b599c74a3c3ce1a1b0c2ce11dda57a4e19490197","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq4","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"RESOLUTION MECHANICS FIRST — this is the CQ where the definition does the most work. The stated measure is \"10y nominal benchmark minus 10y inflation swap / TIPS breakeven\", GDP-weighted across G7. Five audit findings:\n(1) TWO G7 MEMBERS CANNOT BE MEASURED AS STATED. Canada discontinued Real Return Bonds in Nov 2022 and has no liquid 10y CAD inflation swap market; Japan's JGBi breakevens are thin and BOJ-distorted. So ~16% of G7 GDP requires a proxy the question does not specify. Any resolver's proxy choice moves the aggregate 5-15bp.\n(2) BREAKEVEN IS NOT EXPECTED INFLATION. It embeds a positive inflation risk premium (10-30bp when inflation uncertainty is high, exactly the CQ5 regime) and a positive TIPS liquidity premium. Nominal-minus-breakeven therefore OVERSTATES the ex-ante real rate by roughly 10-40bp in the states of the world this panel is forecasting. Direction: the measured CQ4 prints HIGH relative to any structural r*.\n(3) UK RPI REFORM IS A STEP DISCONTINUITY INSIDE THE WINDOW. UK breakevens are RPI-linked and carry an RPI-CPI wedge of ~80-100bp; the 2030 alignment of RPI to CPIH collapses that wedge, mechanically RAISING the UK measured real yield by ~0.6-1.0pp from 2030 with no change in the economics. At ~8% of G7 PPP GDP that is +5-8bp on the aggregate and a visible break in the series.\n(4) WEIGHTING BASIS. The scope lock says constant 2025 PPP; PPP weights raise Japan and Italy relative to market-rate weights. Japan is the low-real-yield member and Italy the high one, so the two partly offset, but the net PPP-vs-market difference is ~5-15bp and the question does not say which.\n(5) THE US ANCHOR IS NOT THE SAME OBJECT AS THE US TERM IN CQ4. The locked prior quotes DFII10 (a fitted par TIPS yield) at 2.60%; nominal-minus-breakeven differs from it by indexation-lag, carry and seasonality effects of ~5bp. A minor identity that does not close exactly, but it matters that the panel's anchor and the resolution formula are different series.\nFERMI AT TODAY'S LEVELS. G7-internal 2025 PPP GDP shares: US 51.5%, Japan 11.7%, Germany 10.5%, UK 7.9%, France 7.6%, Italy 6.2%, Canada 4.6%. Plugging plausible 2032-36 member averages (US 2.6, JP 0.6, DE 1.2, UK 2.0 post-RPI-reform, FR 1.8, IT 2.6, CA 2.2) gives 1.34+0.07+0.13+0.16+0.14+0.16+0.10 ≈ 2.09%. So simply freezing the current configuration yields ~2.1%, with Japan the only material drag.\nSTRUCTURE. Up: 2A's +1.0pp worsening in global (r-g); gross public debt >100% of GDP by 2029 (US ~142%, China ~127% by 2031); net interest 2%->3% of GDP; P95 Debt-at-Risk 117% (+2.4pp under a 20% AI equity shock); eroding sovereign convenience yields; levered basis-trade absorption replacing price-insensitive official demand; retiree dissaving shrinking the saver pool while AI capex and grid buildout bid for it. Down: post-bust excess capacity, BIS Box C labour-income demand shortfall, flight-to-quality episodes inside the window, and the possibility of explicit financial repression (which would compress the measured real yield while the fiscal problem worsens — a regime where CQ4 prints LOW precisely because the stress is severe).\nIMPORTANT CROSS-REFERENCE TRAP: the IMF's (r-g) uses the EFFECTIVE interest rate on the outstanding debt stock — a backward-looking weighted average coupon — which lags the 10y marginal real yield by 100-150bp during a rate-rise phase because of the maturity roll. A reader who equates CQ4 with the IMF's r in 2A will double-count. P50 = 2.00%; P10 = 1.00% (secular-stagnation/repression state); P90 = 3.10% (term-premium/fiscal-dominance state), with the measure's own upward biases (2) and (3) keeping the distribution right-of-centre relative to a true ex-ante real rate.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:52.351Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Eurostat publishes the 2026 Structure of Earnings Survey microdata with median gross hourly earnings by NACE section, and the 2022-to-2026 change in NACE Q median hourly earnings is NEGATIVE or below the all-sector median change in at least 8 of the 12 largest EU members, despite published collective-agreement wage floors for care staff rising faster than the all-economy average in those members.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029 (SES 2026 reference year, publication typically ~30-36 months after)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"CONFIRMS my central CQ1 claim (entrant dilution of the Q median) and would push CQ1 from 6 toward 3-4. The inverse observation — Q median hourly earnings outpacing the all-sector median by >=4pp over 2022-26 in most of those members — falsifies my composition argument and would roughly double CQ1 to 11-13, and would also raise CQ6-C by ~3pp since it implies the care wage channel reaches the core index.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"A published OECD or BIS decomposition of G7 core CPI/PCE attributes >=0.40pp/yr of 2027-2029 core inflation to electricity and grid-cost pass-through through non-energy channels (business-cost pass-through into services and processed food), i.e. the data-centre energy shock demonstrably crosses the core exclusion boundary at a scale above my +0.1-0.2pp estimate.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2030\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my 'core excludes the AI energy shock' driver, which is doing most of the work holding CQ6-C down. Would raise CQ6-C from 17 to ~26 (taken from B), raise the OECD leg of CQ2 from 62% to ~72% and CQ2 from 39 to ~45, and raise the C-consistent part of CQ4's distribution (P50 2.0 -> ~2.2).\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China's NBS publishes a rebased CPI basket (2026 base) under which 2027 and 2028 annual headline CPI both print >=1.8%, with the increase traceable to services and rent weights rather than to a food/pork cycle (i.e. core-equivalent services CPI >=2.0%), and household consumption rises above 42% of GDP in the same national accounts vintage.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2029\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my treatment of Chinese CPI as structurally floored near zero by the property adjustment, the low household income share and supply-side industrial policy. Drops the China conjunct of CQ2 from 72% to ~45% and CQ2 from 39 to ~26. Also raises my CQ3 China leg by ~0.4pp (P50 1.8 -> ~2.0) and shifts ~3pp from CQ6-D toward B.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"An equity drawdown of >=20% (MSCI World, USD, closing basis) occurs in which the G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal sovereign yield FALLS by >=100bp between the equity peak date and the trough date — i.e. the classic flight-to-quality reasserts itself despite the 2A plumbing (convenience-yield erosion, basis-trade absorption, >100% debt/GDP).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the fiscal-dominance correlation structure behind CQ5. Drops P(yield condition | drawdown) from ~0.50 to ~0.30 and CQ5 from 27 to ~17; also lowers my CQ4 P90 from 3.1 to ~2.7 and shifts ~3pp from CQ6-C to CQ6-D (bond-market tolerance means repression is unnecessary and inflation is less likely to be accommodated).\"} | {\"signpost\":\"ICE announces a methodology change to, re-basing of, or discontinuation of the BofA MOVE Index, OR publishes a relative-volatility (log/percentage) variant that becomes the quoted benchmark; alternatively, Eurostat formally incorporates owner-occupied housing into the headline HICP with an announced start date inside the